WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.110
ORM models are application-facing representations of database tables,
records, and relationships.

2
00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:14.256
They can make domain concepts visible in code: users, tenants,
organizations, projects, invoices, messages, files, roles, and events.

3
00:00:14.256 --> 00:00:17.346
Model design should reflect more than column names.

4
00:00:17.346 --> 00:00:26.546
It should capture ownership, sensitivity, lifecycle, relationships, and
business rules that affect how data is created, changed, queried, and
exposed.

5
00:00:26.546 --> 00:00:30.689
Data flowing into models can come from trusted and untrusted sources.

6
00:00:30.689 --> 00:00:42.417
Request bodies, form fields, API payloads, uploaded files, imported
spreadsheets, partner responses, administrative edits, and background jobs
may all influence model values or query choices.

7
00:00:42.417 --> 00:00:46.912
Stored data can later be reused in queries, reports, exports, or
templates.

8
00:00:46.912 --> 00:00:51.617
A value should not become trusted merely because it has been saved to a
database.

9
00:00:51.617 --> 00:00:54.004
Validation boundaries should be clear.

10
00:00:54.004 --> 00:00:59.693
Some rules belong at the request or form layer because they depend on the
action being performed.

11
00:00:59.693 --> 00:01:03.977
Some rules belong near the model because they protect data consistency.

12
00:01:03.977 --> 00:01:11.842
Some rules belong in the service or repository layer because they combine
identity, permissions, workflow state, and persistence.

13
00:01:11.842 --> 00:01:19.497
Teams should be able to explain which layer enforces which assumption
instead of hoping the ORM enforces everything implicitly.

14
00:01:19.497 --> 00:01:25.115
Service and repository layer concepts can help keep database access
explicit and reviewable.

15
00:01:25.115 --> 00:01:40.986
Instead of scattering complex queries across views, jobs, serializers, and
admin actions, teams can centralize sensitive operations in named
functions that include validation, authorization, tenant filtering,
transaction behavior, and logging expectations.

16
00:01:40.986 --> 00:01:48.360
The exact architecture can vary, but safe ORM design makes important data
flows easy to find and hard to bypass accidentally.
